WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.310
Module eleven:

2
00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:02.339
honest testing.

3
00:00:02.339 --> 00:00:07.070
How a backtest lies, how peeking into the future inflates the result,

4
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:12.350
why the best of 500 random variants proves nothing, and why data is held out.

5
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:18.297
A backtest is a calculation of what a rule would have earned if it had been traded

6
00:00:18.297 --> 00:00:19.021
in the past.

7
00:00:19.021 --> 00:00:21.494
It is the only cheap way to test an idea.

8
00:00:21.494 --> 00:00:22.580
But it is a model:

9
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:27.286
it assumes correct execution prices, correct fees and a rule known in advance.

10
00:00:27.286 --> 00:00:30.966
Each of these assumptions can be broken without you noticing.

11
00:00:31.966 --> 00:00:34.503
The most common mistake is one candle:

12
00:00:34.503 --> 00:00:39.376
the rule looks at the day's close and buys at the start of that same day.

13
00:00:39.376 --> 00:00:41.645
In code it is a shift of one line.

14
00:00:41.645 --> 00:00:44.315
The blue line is the honest calculation:

15
00:00:44.315 --> 00:00:45.450
a sevenfold rise.

16
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.922
The red one gets the gain of the very day the decision was made on:

17
00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:50.590
176 times.

18
00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:52.764
Take 500 monkeys.

19
00:00:52.764 --> 00:00:57.875
Each one, day after day, randomly decides whether to be in bitcoin or not.

20
00:00:57.875 --> 00:00:59.188
No analysis at all.

21
00:00:59.188 --> 00:01:02.089
Let us run them on real history with fees.

22
00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:06.302
The best ones look like geniuses, although it is pure chance.

23
00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:10.697
So the best of many variants proves nothing.

24
00:01:10.697 --> 00:01:13.706
Its t-statistic is compared with a bar:

25
00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:18.722
what the best of the same number of variants of pure noise gives.

26
00:01:18.722 --> 00:01:23.814
For one attempt it is 1.96, for 20 it is 2.45, for 500 it is 3.53.

27
00:01:24.814 --> 00:01:26.001
All attempts count:

28
00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:30.813
every parameter, every coin, every variant of the rule, even a discarded one.

29
00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:35.563
That is why the program keeps an attempts journal and raises the bar itself.

30
00:01:35.563 --> 00:01:40.750
The honest number of attempts is almost always larger than you would like to admit.

31
00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.769
Overfitting is when a rule has memorized the noise of the past.

32
00:01:45.769 --> 00:01:47.810
The protection is held-out data.

33
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.702
History is split into a sandbox, where you may tune anything,

34
00:01:51.702 --> 00:01:54.253
and a held-out part that is opened once.

35
00:01:54.253 --> 00:01:58.910
The sandbox's best monkey behaves like a random one on the held-out data.

36
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.275
Real rules are the same, only milder.

37
00:02:02.275 --> 00:02:06.174
The rule “hold above the average” with 30 variants of length:

38
00:02:06.174 --> 00:02:09.114
the best variant in the sandbox grew 36 times,

39
00:02:09.114 --> 00:02:11.926
and on held-out data a little more than two,

40
00:02:11.926 --> 00:02:14.738
slightly better than the median and holding.

41
00:02:14.738 --> 00:02:18.126
The sandbox's promise is exaggerated many times over.

42
00:02:19.126 --> 00:02:20.869
Fees, slippage, funding:

43
00:02:20.869 --> 00:02:24.645
small percentages, but they are paid on every trade,

44
00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:27.695
and a rule's edge is usually even smaller.

45
00:02:27.695 --> 00:02:33.431
The turtles on bitcoin made 136 trades and, with fees raised to half a percent,

46
00:02:33.431 --> 00:02:35.174
lost half of the result.

47
00:02:35.174 --> 00:02:38.950
A rule with 40 trades hardly noticed the difference.

48
00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.700
The order of honest testing.

49
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.637
Write the rule down in advance.

50
00:02:43.637 --> 00:02:44.824
Count all attempts.

51
00:02:44.824 --> 00:02:46.574
Open the held-out data once.

52
00:02:46.574 --> 00:02:49.573
And only then, watch it on paper, without money.

53
00:02:49.573 --> 00:02:53.822
That is exactly how the stages of the program's work mode are built.

54
00:02:54.822 --> 00:02:55.885
The main points.

55
00:02:55.885 --> 00:03:01.466
A backtest easily lies through peeking into the future, overfitting and wrong costs.

56
00:03:01.466 --> 00:03:05.984
Among hundreds of random strategies there is always a brilliant one,

57
00:03:05.984 --> 00:03:08.974
so the bar rises with the number of attempts.

58
00:03:08.974 --> 00:03:11.166
And held-out data is opened once.
